**What changed:** The configuration metadata annotation processor in Spring Boot 3.3.0 now supports generating descriptions for record components in `@ConfigurationProperties` record classes. Previously, there was no way to automatically generate descriptions for record components because records do not have explicit fields for field-level Javadoc. Now, descriptions are extracted from the class-level Javadoc `@param` tags for the record's components. This enhancement was implemented in [spring-projects/spring-boot#29403](https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/pull/29403) and included in the 3.3.0 release ([v3.3.0 release notes](https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/releases/tag/v3.3.0)).

**Affects:** Applications using `@ConfigurationProperties` with Java record classes that rely on the configuration metadata processor to generate `spring-configuration-metadata.json` descriptions.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```java
// Before (3.2.x): Descriptions from class-level @param tags were not picked up
/**
 * Some description.
 * @param myProperty The description for myProperty.
 */
@ConfigurationProperties("app")
public record MyProperties(String myProperty) {
}

// After (3.3.x): Descriptions from class-level @param tags are now extracted for metadata generation
/**
 * Some description.
 * @param myProperty The description for myProperty.
 */
@ConfigurationProperties("app")
public record MyProperties(String myProperty) {
}
```

**What changed:** In Spring Boot 3.3.0, `PulsarPropertiesMapper.getAuthenticationParamsJson(...)` (in `org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.pulsar.PulsarPropertiesMapper`) now JSON-escapes the values of the `spring.pulsar.client.authentication.param` map before building the auth params JSON string passed to `AuthenticationFactory.create(authPluginClassName, authParams)`. Previously (early 3.2.x, after commit `41ed4d6` "Remove use of Pulsar ObjectMapperFactory"), values were interpolated raw into `"\"%s\":\"%s\""`, producing invalid JSON when a value contained characters such as `"`, `\`, `/`, `\t` or `\n`. The fix (forward port of PR #40493 for issue #40492, tracked as #40869 for 3.3.x) adds a hand-rolled `escapeJson` method that escapes `\\`, `\"`, `/`, `\b`, `\t`, `\n`, `\f`, `\r` (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/40869, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/40493, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/v3.3.0/spring-boot-project/spring-boot-autoconfigure/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/autoconfigure/pulsar/PulsarPropertiesMapper.java).

**Affects:** Applications that set `spring.pulsar.client.authentication.plugin-class-name` together with `spring.pulsar.client.authentication.param.*` entries whose values contain JSON special characters (e.g. base64 secrets with `/` or `+`, embedded quotes), particularly with custom auth plugins; also affects the same properties under `spring.pulsar.client.failover.backup-clusters[*].authentication.param`.

**Action:** none — this is a bug fix applied automatically; no configuration or code change is required. Re-verify that custom Pulsar auth plugins still receive the expected `authParams` JSON, since parameter values containing special characters will now arrive JSON-escaped (e.g. `/` becomes `\/`).

**Before/after:**
```java
// before (raw interpolation, invalid JSON for special chars)
.map((entry) -> "\"%s\":\"%s\"".formatted(entry.getKey(), entry.getValue()))

// after 3.3.0 (value escaped)
.map((entry) -> "\"%s\":\"%s\"".formatted(entry.getKey(), escapeJson(entry.getValue())))

private String escapeJson(String raw) {
    return raw.replace("\\", "\\\\").replace("\"", "\\\"").replace("/", "\\/")
        .replace("\b", "\\b").replace("\t", "\\t").replace("\n", "\\n")
        .replace("\f", "\\f").replace("\r", "\\r");
}
```

**What changed:** In Spring Boot 3.2.x, `@SpyBean` fails to initialize `MongoRepository` beans with generic type parameters. This is a regression introduced in 3.2.x that was reported as issue #40234 and forward-ported to 3.3.x as issue #40855. The bug prevents `@SpyBean` from correctly creating spy instances for repositories declared with generics, such as `ExampleRepository<ExampleData>`. The fix for this regression is included in the 3.3.0 release under the Bug Fixes section as "Starting from 3.2.x, @SpyBean is not able to initialise MongoRepository bean of the generic type #40855" (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/40855, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/40234, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/releases/tag/v3.3.0).

**Affects:** Applications using `@SpyBean` on `MongoRepository` subclasses that declare a generic type parameter (e.g., `private ExampleRepository<ExampleData> exampleRepository;`).

**Action:** none (the issue is fixed in 3.3.0; applications affected in 3.2.x will work correctly after upgrading. As a temporary workaround on 3.2.x, remove the generic type from the field declaration.)

**Before/after:**
```java
// Workaround on 3.2.x (remove generic type):
// Before (fails on 3.2.x):
@SpyBean
private ExampleRepository<ExampleData> exampleRepository;

// After (workaround on 3.2.x; not needed on 3.3.0):
@SpyBean
private ExampleRepository exampleRepository;
```

Based on the retrieved sources, specifically the diff in PR #40836, the change involves a typo fix in the bean method name within `Neo4jReactiveDataAutoConfiguration`.

**What changed:** The bean method name in `Neo4jReactiveDataAutoConfiguration` was corrected from `rectiveNeo4jTransactionManager` to `reactiveNeo4jTransactionManager` to fix a typo. Previously, the bean would have been registered with the name `rectiveNeo4jTransactionManager` because Spring uses the method name as the default bean name when no explicit name is provided in the `@Bean` annotation (the annotation `@Bean(ReactiveNeo4jRepositoryConfigurationExtension.DEFAULT_TRANSACTION_MANAGER_BEAN_NAME)` explicitly sets the name, but the method name change aligns the code with standard naming conventions and potentially avoids confusion if the explicit name attribute were ever changed or removed). Wait, looking closely at the diff: `@Bean(ReactiveNeo4jRepositoryConfigurationExtension.DEFAULT_TRANSACTION_MANAGER_BEAN_NAME)` is present in both before and after. The `@Bean` annotation explicitly defines the bean name as `ReactiveNeo4jRepositoryConfigurationExtension.DEFAULT_TRANSACTION_MANAGER_BEAN_NAME`. The change is purely cosmetic in the Java source method name `rectiveNeo4jTransactionManager` → `reactiveNeo4jTransactionManager`. It does not change the actual Spring bean name.

**Affects:** none (internal code quality/typo fix only, no runtime behavioral change for applications unless they are reflectively invoking this specific private/package-private method by name, which is unlikely).

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```java
// Before
@Bean(ReactiveNeo4jRepositoryConfigurationExtension.DEFAULT_TRANSACTION_MANAGER_BEAN_NAME)
@ConditionalOnMissingBean(ReactiveTransactionManager.class)
ReactiveNeo4jTransactionManager rectiveNeo4jTransactionManager(Driver driver,
        ReactiveDatabaseSelectionProvider databaseNameProvider) {
    return new ReactiveNeo4jTransactionManager(driver, databaseNameProvider);
}

// After
@Bean(ReactiveNeo4jRepositoryConfigurationExtension.DEFAULT_TRANSACTION_MANAGER_BEAN_NAME)
@ConditionalOnMissingBean(ReactiveTransactionManager.class)
ReactiveNeo4jTransactionManager reactiveNeo4jTransactionManager(Driver driver,
        ReactiveDatabaseSelectionProvider databaseNameProvider) {
    return new ReactiveNeo4jTransactionManager(driver, databaseNameProvider);
}
```

**What changed:** The internal `ReflectiveClientHttpRequestFactoryBuilder` in `ClientHttpRequestFactories` attempts to configure read timeouts by reflectively invoking a `setReadTimeout(int)` method on the underlying `ClientHttpRequestFactory`. However, `JettyClientHttpRequestFactory` (from Spring Framework) exposes `setReadTimeout(long)` and `setReadTimeout(Duration)` methods, but not a variant accepting `int`. Consequently, when `JettyClientHttpRequestFactory` is used, the reflective lookup fails to find the expected method signature, and the read timeout configured via Spring Boot properties (e.g., `spring.http.client.read-timeout`) is not applied. (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/40635)

**Affects:** Applications using `JettyClientHttpRequestFactory` as the underlying client for `RestClient` or `RestTemplate` configured via Spring Boot's auto-configuration properties, specifically when attempting to set a read timeout.

**Action:** unclear from the sources. The issue confirms the bug exists, but the fetched pages do not describe the specific fix implemented in 3.3.0 (e.g., whether the reflective builder was updated to support `Duration` or `long` parameters, or if users must configure timeouts manually on the factory instance).

**Before/after:**
```java
// In ClientHttpRequestFactories.java (Reflective factory logic)
// Old behavior (approximate):
// Attempts to invoke setReadTimeout(int timeout) via reflection.
// Fails for JettyClientHttpRequestFactory which has setReadTimeout(Duration).

// New behavior (inferred from issue resolution pattern, not explicit in fetched snippets):
// Should attempt to invoke setReadTimeout(Duration duration) or setReadTimeout(long millis)
// if the int variant is not found.
```

**What changed:** In 3.2.x (pre-3.2.6), when `AotDetector.useGeneratedArtifacts()` is true and the expected `__ApplicationContextInitializer` class is not found, `SpringApplication.addAotGeneratedInitializerIfNecessary` threw a generic `IllegalArgumentException` with the message: "You are starting the application with AOT mode enabled but AOT processing hasn't happened. Please build your application with enabled AOT processing first, or remove the system property 'spring.aot.enabled' to run the application in regular mode" (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/pull/38188, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/v3.2.5/spring-boot-project/spring-boot/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/SpringApplication.java). Starting in 3.3.0, the code throws a dedicated `AotInitializerNotFoundException` (added in 3.2.6, forward-ported to 3.3.0 via https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/40584) with the message: "Startup with AOT mode enabled failed: AOT initializer %s could not be found" (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/v3.3.0/spring-boot-project/spring-boot/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/AotInitializerNotFoundException.java). Issue #40584 notes that this new message is sometimes less helpful than the old one for identifying the root cause of problems like Kotlin `@JvmStatic` main method incompatibilities (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/40584).

**Affects:** Applications that run with AOT processing enabled (`spring.aot.enabled=true`) but where the AOT-generated initializer class (`<mainClass>__ApplicationContextInitializer`) cannot be found. This includes applications that have not been AOT-processed despite having the property set, or where AOT processing failed silently.

**Action:** none. No code or configuration changes are required; however, if your application was relying on catching `IllegalArgumentException` from `SpringApplication.addAotGeneratedInitializerIfNecessary`, note that it now throws `AotInitializerNotFoundException` (a subclass of `RuntimeException`). Applications that were not handling this exception specifically are unaffected.

**Before/after:**
```java
// Before (3.2.x):
throw new IllegalArgumentException(
    "You are starting the application with AOT mode enabled but AOT processing hasn't happened. "
        + "Please build your application with enabled AOT processing first, "
        + "or remove the system property 'spring.aot.enabled' to run the application in regular mode");

// After (3.3.0):
throw new AotInitializerNotFoundException(this.mainApplicationClass, initializerClassName);
// Message: "Startup with AOT mode enabled failed: AOT initializer <name> could not be found"
```

**What changed:** In Spring Boot 3.3.0, the `TestcontainersLifecycleBeanPostProcessor` was fixed to correctly shut down containers between tests when `container.withReuse(true)` is used but the environment does not support reuse (e.g., `testcontainers.reuse.enabled=true` is missing in `testcontainers.properties`). Previously, `isShouldBeReused()` returned `true` based solely on the `.withReuse()` call, causing `TestcontainersLifecycleBeanPostProcessor.postProcessBeforeDestruction()` to skip shutdowns even when reuse was disabled by the environment, leading to resource exhaustion and stuck CI builds (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/39609, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/40509, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/releases/tag/v3.3.0).
**Affects:** Applications using Testcontainers integration (via `@Testcontainers`, `ImportTestcontainers`, or `@Container` definitions) that call `.withReuse(true)` on containers in environments where `testcontainers.reuse.enabled=true` is not set in `testcontainers.properties`.
**Action:** none
**Before/after:**
```java
// No code/configuration change required for the fix itself.
// The issue describes a previous workaround for the bug, which is no longer needed:

// Old Workaround (unnecessary after fix):
// if (TestcontainerConfiguration.getInstance().environmentSupportsReuse()) {
//     container.withReuse(true);
// }

// Current behavior:
// container.withReuse(true) works safely even if reuse is disabled in the environment,
// as containers are now properly shut down when reuse is not supported.
```

I have sufficient facts from the two GitHub issues (#40501 forward-port of #39766), which are the authoritative sources for this change.

**What changed:** A `CookieSameSiteSupplier` bean now also influences the SameSite attribute of the session cookie. This behavioural regression (originally reported for 3.2, introduced with the upgrade to Jetty 10, where session cookie creation goes through `SuppliedSameSiteCookieHandlerWrapper`) was forward-ported to 3.3.x as issue #40501 (type: regression, status: forward-port). In Spring Boot 2.7, only the `server.servlet.session.cookie.same-site` configuration property affected the session cookie (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/40501, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/39766). The reference documentation previously implied the supplier applied only to "other cookies added to your `HttpServletResponse`", i.e. not the session cookie (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/39766).

**Affects:** Applications that define a `CookieSameSiteSupplier` bean and use a servlet container (notably Jetty) — the SameSite attribute applied to the session cookie (e.g. `JSESSIONID`) may change compared to 2.7/before.

**Action:** required: re-verify the SameSite attribute emitted on the session cookie. If a `CookieSameSiteSupplier` bean must not affect it, adapt the supplier to exclude the session cookie by name (as suggested by the issue reporter), and keep configuring the session cookie via `server.servlet.session.cookie.same-site`.

**Before/after:**

```java
// Old: any supplier rule also applied to the session cookie
CookieSameSiteSupplier.ofLax().whenMatchingTo(".*");

// Mitigation from the issue: exclude the session cookie by name
// (adapt the CookieSameSiteSupplier bean to exclude the session cookie)
```

**What changed:** In 3.2.x, `BindableRuntimeHintsRegistrar.registerHints(RuntimeHints)` collected bind-constructor types whose parameter names could not be discovered (classes compiled without `-parameters`) into a `compiledWithoutParameters` set and then threw `org.springframework.boot.context.properties.bind.MissingParametersCompilerArgumentException`, aborting AOT processing / native-image build — even when the offending class came from a third-party library (e.g. `org.apache.tomcat.jdbc.pool.PoolProperties$InterceptorProperty` when using `tomcat-jdbc`) and the app worked fine without AOT (#40051, forward-ported as #40485/#40486). In 3.3.0 the `ParameterNameDiscoverer`-based `verifyParameterNamesAreAvailable()` check, the `compiledWithoutParameters` set, and the `MissingParametersCompilerArgumentException` throw were removed from `BindableRuntimeHintsRegistrar`; hint registration now proceeds without failing when parameter information is unavailable (v3.2.0 vs v3.3.0 source of `spring-boot-project/spring-boot/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/context/properties/bind/BindableRuntimeHintsRegistrar.java`).

**Affects:** applications using AOT processing or native image where a constructor-bound (value object) `@ConfigurationProperties` type, or a nested property type, is compiled without `-parameters` — previously build failed with `MissingParametersCompilerArgumentException`.

**Action:** none — the fix is automatic; the only follow-up is that native images that build successfully now may lack parameter-name hints, so constructor binding of such classes should be re-verified at runtime in the native image.

**Before/after:**
```java
// 3.2.x — BindableRuntimeHintsRegistrar.registerHints
Set<Class<?>> compiledWithoutParameters = new HashSet<>();
for (Bindable<?> bindable : this.bindables) {
    new Processor(bindable, compiledWithoutParameters).process(hints.reflection());
}
if (!compiledWithoutParameters.isEmpty()) {
    throw new MissingParametersCompilerArgumentException(compiledWithoutParameters);
}

// 3.3.0 — no parameter-name verification, no exception
for (Bindable<?> bindable : this.bindables) {
    new Processor(bindable).process(hints.reflection());
}
```

**What changed:** In Spring Boot 3.2.x, the `PropertiesRedisConnectionDetails.asNode` method parsed entries in `spring.data.redis.cluster.nodes` and `spring.data.redis.sentinel.nodes` using `node.split(":")`. This logic failed for IPv6 addresses formatted as `[::1]:6379`, incorrectly splitting on the internal colons of the address. In Spring Boot 3.3.0, the method was fixed to use `node.lastIndexOf(':')` to identify the port separator, allowing the host portion to contain colons. (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/pull/39819, )

**Affects:** Applications that define IPv6 addresses in the `spring.data.redis.cluster.nodes` or `spring.data.redis.sentinel.nodes` properties.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```java
// 3.2.x (Incorrect for IPv6)
private Node asNode(String node) {
    String[] components = node.split(":");
    return new Node(components[0], Integer.parseInt(components[1]));
}

// 3.3.0 (Correct for IPv6)
private Node asNode(String node) {
    int portSeparatorIndex = node.lastIndexOf(':');
    String host = node.substring(0, portSeparatorIndex);
    int port = Integer.parseInt(node.substring(portSeparatorIndex + 1));
    return new Node(host, port);
}
```

**What changed:** `DevToolsDataSourceAutoConfiguration` now guards against the absence of `javax.sql.DataSource` by adding `@ConditionalOnClass(DataSource.class)` at the class level, and refactored condition evaluation into a custom `DevToolsDataSourceCondition` (extending `SpringBootCondition`) to check for the presence of a single `DataSource` and `DataSourceProperties` bean before registration. Previously, the autoconfiguration assumed the class was available and relied on implicit conditions, causing failures in applications using Spring Boot DevTools without a DataSource on the classpath (e.g., JPMS-based apps or apps with no DataSource) (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/40441) (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/40434)
**Affects:** Applications using `spring-boot-devtools` where `javax.sql.DataSource` is not available on the classpath, or where no auto-configured `DataSource` bean is present.
**Action:** none
**Before/after:**
```java
@AutoConfiguration(after = DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)
@Conditional(OnEnabledDevToolsCondition.class)
public class DevToolsDataSourceAutoConfiguration { ... }
```
```java
@ConditionalOnClass(DataSource.class)
@Conditional({ OnEnabledDevToolsCondition.class, DevToolsDataSourceCondition.class })
@AutoConfiguration(after = DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)
@Import(DatabaseShutdownExecutorEntityManagerFactoryDependsOnPostProcessor.class)
public class DevToolsDataSourceAutoConfiguration { ... }
```

**What changed:** When `spring.config.activate.on-cloud-platform=none` (i.e. `CloudPlatform.NONE`) is set in a multi-document config file's activate section, it now matches when the application's active cloud platform is `null` (the value when no cloud platform is detected and `spring.main.cloud-platform` is not set). Previously this only matched when the active cloud platform explicitly equalled `CloudPlatform.NONE` (which required setting `spring.main.cloud-platform=NONE`). The check in `ConfigDataProperties.Activate#isActive(CloudPlatform)` changed from `this.onCloudPlatform == null || this.onCloudPlatform == cloudPlatform` to additionally return true when `this.onCloudPlatform == CloudPlatform.NONE && cloudPlatform == null` (https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/spring-projects/spring-boot/pull/38510.diff, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38506, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/wiki/Spring-Boot-3.3-Release-Notes).

**Affects:** Applications or libraries that use `spring.config.activate.on-cloud-platform=none` (or `none:`) in `application.properties`/`application.yml` multi-document configs to activate a profile/doc only when not running on a cloud platform, on a run where no cloud platform is detected.

**Action:** none. This is a fix that makes `=none` match the common "not on a cloud platform" case; re-verify that multi-document config profiles previously guarded with `=none` (and previously not activated without `spring.main.cloud-platform=NONE`) now activate as intended.

**Before/after:**
```java
// Before (3.2): only matched exact CloudPlatform value
private boolean isActive(CloudPlatform cloudPlatform) {
    return this.onCloudPlatform == null || this.onCloudPlatform == cloudPlatform;
}

// After (3.3): also matches when requested=NONE and active platform is null
private boolean isActive(CloudPlatform cloudPlatform) {
    return this.onCloudPlatform == null
        || this.onCloudPlatform == CloudPlatform.NONE && cloudPlatform == null
        || this.onCloudPlatform == cloudPlatform;
}
```

**What changed:** Spring Boot 3.3 lists `jakarta.inject:jakarta.inject-api` under "Miscellaneous" changes: "jakarta.inject:jakarta.inject-api is now included in Spring Boot’s dependency management" (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/wiki/Spring-Boot-3.3-Release-Notes). The request came from issue "Manage jakarta.inject:jakarta.inject-api", spring-projects/spring-boot#39587, closed into milestone 3.3.0-M3 (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/39587). The 3.3.0 BOM build now declares `library("Jakarta Inject", "2.0.1") { group("jakarta.inject") { modules = [ "jakarta.inject-api" ] } }`; the 3.2.0 BOM had no "Jakarta Inject" entry, i.e. the coordinates were previously unmanaged (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/v3.3.0/spring-boot-project/spring-boot-dependencies/build.gradle, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/v3.2.0/spring-boot-project/spring-boot-dependencies/build.gradle).

**Affects:** applications that declare a dependency on `jakarta.inject:jakarta.inject-api` (for example when using `@Inject` / `@Named` from the Jakarta Inject API) without an explicit `<version>`/version string; unaffected applications see no change.

**Action:** none — no code or configuration change is required. Optional convenience: a hard-coded version for `jakarta.inject:jakarta.inject-api` can now be dropped so the Spring Boot BOM supplies 2.0.1; verify the version actually resolved in the build, since any pre-existing explicit version still overrides the managed one.

**Before/after:**
```gradle
// Before (3.2.x): no BOM entry for jakarta.inject — a version had to be given
// implementation "jakarta.inject:jakarta.inject-api:<version>"

// Spring Boot 3.3.0 dependencies BOM (v3.3.0 build.gradle)
library("Jakarta Inject", "2.0.1") {
	group("jakarta.inject") {
		modules = [
			"jakarta.inject-api"
		]
	}
}
```

**What changed:** The JPA auto-configuration `PersistenceManagedTypesConfiguration` inner class of `JpaBaseConfiguration` now injects `ObjectProvider<ManagedClassNameFilter> managedClassNameFilter` and constructs the auto-configured `PersistenceManagedTypes` by calling `new PersistenceManagedTypesScanner(resourceLoader, managedClassNameFilter.getIfAvailable()).scan(packagesToScan)`. The filter is the one defined by `org.springframework.orm.jpa.persistenceunit.ManagedClassNameFilter`; the change was introduced to let a bean customize which scanned managed-class names are accepted (originally requested to allow scanning only specific entity classes rather than whole packages) (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/29193, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/39813, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/v3.3.0/spring-boot-project/spring-boot-autoconfigure/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/autoconfigure/orm/jpa/JpaBaseConfiguration.java). This change is not listed in the 3.3 release notes (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/wiki/Spring-Boot-3.3-Release-Notes).

**Affects:** Applications relying on the auto-configured `LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean`/`PersistenceManagedTypes` (i.e., not defining their own `LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean` or `EntityManagerFactory`) that declare or intend to declare a `ManagedClassNameFilter` bean; otherwise the auto-configured scanning behaviour is unchanged (the filter is `getIfAvailable()` and is `null` when absent).

**Action:** none. Existing applications keep the prior behaviour unless they add a `ManagedClassNameFilter` bean, in which case the auto-configured `PersistenceManagedTypesScanner` will apply it to the scanned package results.

**Before/after:**
```java
// New (3.3) in JpaBaseConfiguration.PersistenceManagedTypesConfiguration:
@Bean
@Primary
@ConditionalOnMissingBean
static PersistenceManagedTypes persistenceManagedTypes(BeanFactory beanFactory, ResourceLoader resourceLoader,
        ObjectProvider<ManagedClassNameFilter> managedClassNameFilter) {
    String[] packagesToScan = getPackagesToScan(beanFactory);
    return new PersistenceManagedTypesScanner(resourceLoader, managedClassNameFilter.getIfAvailable())
        .scan(packagesToScan);
}
```

**What changed:** The property `layers.includeLayerTools` on `BootJar` and `BootWar` Gradle tasks is deprecated in favor of the new property `includeTools` (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/wiki/Spring-Boot-3.3-Release-Notes). This change is related to the new CDS support where the `tools` jarmode is added to the executable JAR by default, making layer tools inclusion independent of `layers.enabled` (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/wiki/Spring-Boot-3.3-Release-Notes).

**Affects:** Spring Boot Gradle builds that explicitly set `layers.includeLayerTools = false` to exclude layer tools.

**Action:** required: Replace `layers.includeLayerTools = false` with `includeTools = false` on `bootJar` or `bootWar` task configuration in `build.gradle` (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/wiki/Spring-Boot-3.3-Release-Notes).

**Before/after:**
```gradle
// Before (3.2.x)
bootJar {
    layers {
        includeLayerTools = false
    }
}

// After (3.3+)
bootJar {
    includeTools = false
}
```

**What changed:** The `@ServletEndpoint`, `@ControllerEndpoint`, and `@RestControllerEndpoint` annotations used to declare Actuator endpoints have been deprecated as of Spring Boot 3.3.0. Applications are now directed to use the `@Endpoint`/`@ReadOperation`/`@WriteOperation` model. The `@RestControllerEndpoint` specifically is marked as deprecated in favor of `@Endpoint` and `@WebEndpoint` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/v3.3.0/spring-boot-project/spring-boot-actuator/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/actuate/endpoint/web/annotation/RestControllerEndpoint.java).

**Affects:** All applications using `@ServletEndpoint`, `@ControllerEndpoint`, or `@RestControllerEndpoint` to define custom Actuator endpoints.

**Action:** required: Replace custom endpoint definitions using `@ServletEndpoint`, `@ControllerEndpoint`, or `@RestControllerEndpoint` with the standard `@Endpoint` (or `@WebEndpoint`) annotation, using `@ReadOperation`, `@WriteOperation`, and `@DeleteOperation` annotations on the corresponding methods.

**Before/after:**
```java
// Before (Deprecated in 3.3.0)
@RestControllerEndpoint(id = "example")
public class ExampleEndpoint {
    @GetMapping("/foo")
    @ResponseBody
    public String foo() { ... }
}

// After (Recommended)
@Endpoint(id = "example")
public class ExampleEndpoint {
    @ReadOperation
    public String foo() { ... }
}
```

**What changed:** In Spring Boot 3.2, the dependency-management BOM imported the Dropwizard Metrics BOM: `library("Dropwizard Metrics", "4.2.22") { group("io.dropwizard.metrics") { imports = [ "metrics-bom" ] } }` in `spring-boot-dependencies` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/v3.2.0/spring-boot-project/spring-boot-dependencies/build.gradle). In 3.3.0 this library entry is absent from the BOM (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/v3.3.0/spring-boot-project/spring-boot-dependencies/build.gradle), and the release notes state: "Dependency management for Dropwizard Metrics has been removed. Spring Boot does not depend directly upon Dropwizard Metrics and, therefore, does not require a specific version. If your application depends on Dropwizard Metrics directly, update your build configuration to specify a version that meets its needs." (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/wiki/Spring-Boot-3.3-Release-Notes)
**Affects:** Applications that declare a Dropwizard Metrics dependency (`io.dropwizard.metrics:*`, e.g. via the `metrics-bom` import) without an explicit `<version>`, relying on Spring Boot’s `spring-boot-dependencies` BOM to supply the version. Not applicable when the version is set explicitly or pinned in the project.
**Action:** required: Add an explicit version to the application’s Dropwizard Metrics dependency declarations in its build configuration (build will otherwise fail to resolve the version). No replacement BOM/import or alternative property is named in the release notes or either BOM, so choose the version appropriate to the application.

**Before/after:**
```gradle
// spring-boot-dependencies build.gradle (3.2.0, present; absent in 3.3.0)
library("Dropwizard Metrics", "4.2.22") {
    group("io.dropwizard.metrics") {
        imports = [
            "metrics-bom"
        ]
    }
}
```
